messages:
support_engineer_anna: Heads up — we have a customer reporting duplicate POSTs (duplicate payments) when their calls hit transient 429/500 and our SDK retries. They say it happens intermittently, roughly every few hundred requests. Node app is on Node 20, using @redwood/sdk-js v1.3.0. Logs attached below.
customer_matt: repro snippet from their app:
```
const controller = new AbortController();
// reused controller across many outgoing requests
await Promise.all(items.map(item =>
  client.createCompletion({input: item}, {signal: controller.signal})
));
```
customer_matt: they also said they saw axios-like errors: "FetchError: request aborted" then immediately see our server receive duplicate POSTs for the same payload (no idempotency header).
eng_chris: thanks — couple quick questions: which undici/fetch polyfill is in use? any proxy? Also do they set any custom retry/backoff config in the SDK?
support_engineer_anna: they confirmed no custom retry config (using default retries). undici version shows in package-lock: "undici": "5.14.1". They run in a container on GKE with autoscaling.
eng_chris: ok that undici version is a little old for Node 20's global fetch changes. Two plausible causes: 1) AbortController signal reuse across retries can interact poorly with undici's internal retry/backoff leading to aborted native sockets but the SDK still attempting an application-level retry that results in duplicate downstream POSTs. 2) Missing idempotency on POSTs — the safest short-term mitigation is to require callers to pass an idempotency key for mutating endpoints.
devex_sam: short-term workaround: disable automatic retries for that endpoint and do explicit retry logic with per-attempt AbortController and a jittered backoff. Example:
```
// per-request controller
function send(item) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  return client.createCompletion({input: item}, {signal: controller.signal, retries: 0});
}
```
devex_sam: also set header `Idempotency-Key` so the server can dedupe.
customer_matt: tried disabling retries (retries: 0) and creating a new AbortController per request — appears to stop duplicates in their small test. Still want a permanent fix.
eng_chris: filing an internal bug — looks like the SDK's retry wrapper is reusing the same AbortSignal across attempts in v1.3.0 which is wrong. We'll patch to create a fresh signal per attempt and ensure undici request objects are properly aborted/cancelled before emitting another attempt.
eng_chris: for immediate customer action: upgrade to @redwood/sdk-js v1.3.2 which contains the retry/signal fix and bump undici to >=5.16.0. If they can't upgrade, set `retries: 0` and use per-call AbortController + Idempotency-Key as mentioned.
support-bot: Ticket created: #SUP-2147 linked to internal issue RED-5123. Assigned to eng_chris. :white_check_mark:
customer_matt: thanks — I'll schedule a controlled upgrade and monitor. Can you also send the exact patch/change so our ops can review?
eng_chris: posted the diff in the private eng PR and pinned the changelog snippet: "Fix: do not reuse AbortSignal across retry attempts; ensure safe abort semantics with undici fetch paths". I'll DM the patch link.
support_engineer_anna: marking this thread resolved for support; monitoring for the customer's upgrade. :eyes:
